content.text:
Now 60°/s for 1s gives 64° turn. Great.

But if device rotates 90° over 1s, view turns 90°. Fine.

Now screen orientation mapping sign may be wrong for landscape; test with orientation angle 90 by emulating `screen.orientation.angle`? Can't easily change readonly. Could override `screen.orientation`? Use Object.defineProperty on screen.orientation.angle maybe. But not necessary.

Need test iOS permission denied and unsupported. We can simulate by replacing DeviceMotionEvent? For unsupported, set `window.DeviceMotionEvent = undefined`? But it's a property maybe writable. Then enableTilt -> unsupported. For denied, set requestPermission function returns 'denied'. Could test code path. Do later.

Now balance. Need play through all waves. Could simulate with an autoplayer that kills enemies as they spawn. But we need verify progression and boss. We can write a browser eval that auto-kills each enemy periodically, and check waves. This tests wave progression, boss spawn, win. But we already tested boss. We can simulate wave transitions. Need ensure spawn queue works, wave count increments, intermission. Could accelerate timers. We can set `g.spawnTimer=0`, kill enemies, etc. Let's design eval loop that runs for e.g. 60 seconds game time but we can't speed loop easily; can manipulate `g.updateWaves(1)` and `g.enemies.update(0)` manually, but game loop also runs. We can call `g.updateWaves(0.1)` repeatedly while killing enemies. However game loop running concurrently. Fine.

Alternatively use game loop and auto-kill via setInterval. But browser eval returns promise after some seconds. Could run:
```
const g=window.__saci; g.state='playing'; g.wave=0; g.waveActive=false; g.intermission=0.1; ...
for (let i=0;i<200;i++){ g.updateWaves(0.05); g.enemies.list.forEach(e=>{ if(!e.dead) e.takeDamage(999,e.center())}); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,50)); }
```
But game loop also updates and may spawn. We can control. Need ensure boss doesn't kill player; set player invuln=999 or health 100 each loop. Set `g.player.invuln=999`. Then wave progression. Boss takes 999 damage, triggers death over 2.8s; updateWaves doesn't wait for boss. onBossDefeated triggers. But we need ensure all waves reach 5. We can track.

However manipulating `g.wave` and `waveActive` may conflict with game loop. Better use `g.startGame`, then `g.player.invuln=999`, and auto-kill via `setInterval`. Wait through waves with real time ~ maybe 5 waves * spawn intervals + intermissions = 8+8+8+9 + boss = ~45s. Too long but possible. We can accelerate game time by calling `g.tick(dt)` manually? The game loop also runs. We could set `g.state='paused'` and manually tick? `tick` only updates when playing. Could call `g.updateWaves(1)` repeatedly and `g.enemies.update(0.1)`; no need render. We can simulate 1000 steps in eval quickly. Need avoid game loop interference by set state='paused'? But updateWaves returns if state != playing. We can set state='playing' but pause loop? Could override `g.tick`? Not.

Simpler: use real game and speed up time by modifying `WAVES`? Not exported. Could set `g.currentWave.interval=0`, `g.spawnTimer=0`, `g.intermission=0` each loop. Auto-kill enemies. Then wave progresses quickly. The game loop runs at 60fps, but we can also manually call updateWaves in a loop. Let's use a JS interval inside page that every 30ms kills all enemies and sets spawnTimer=0, intermission=0. It will progress in a few seconds. Track `g.wave`. Use promise resolves when state win. But game loop also updates. Player invuln.

Let's do after current test. Need reload to reset. Use eval with Promise loop:
```
const g=window.__saci;
await g.startGame(); wait 800;
g.player.invuln=999; g.player.health=100;
g.wave=0; g.waveActive=false; g.intermission=0.05;
const t0=performance.now();
while (performance.now()-t0 < 15000 && g.state==='playing') {
  g.player.invuln=999;
  for (const e of [...g.enemies.list]) if (!e.dead && e.type!=='boss') e.takeDamage(999,e.center());
  const b=g.enemies.boss; if (b && !b.dying && !b.dead) { b.hp=1; b.takeDamage(999,b.center()); }
  g.spawnTimer=0; g.intermission=0;
  await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,80));
}
return {wave:g.wave, state:g.state, score:g.score, ...}
```
But `g.spawnTimer=0` each loop, so all spawn queue spawns quickly, and `updateWaves` sees aliveCount maybe after kill, marks wave clear. Good. For boss wave, `spawnBoss` then `spawnTimer=999`; our loop sets spawnTimer=0 but queue empty. Then boss killed. onBossDefeated after 2.8s. We need wait. Could loop until state win or 20s. Fine.

Let's test.

